WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Welcome back to mCoding. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.071
If you had to pick, what would&nbsp;you say 
 is your most disliked&nbsp;dunder method in Python?

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:13.680
Maybe dunder new and&nbsp;all of the 
 metaclass glory that goes with that?

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.480
Or maybe you've seen my video about 
 plus&nbsp;equals and don't like the in-place methods?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.652
Or hey, nobody likes taking the 
 time to write&nbsp;a wrapper, maybe that's it?

00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:24.205
Well, I've got a different choice.

00:00:24.205 --> 00:00:26.920
One that's so disliked in&nbsp;the Python community

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.243
that if you so much as ask 
 what it does on Stack Overflow,

00:00:30.243 --> 00:00:34.480
you will be&nbsp;inundated with 
 comments telling you not to use it.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.737
What dunder is it that could 
 possibly harbor&nbsp;this much hate?

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.960
It's none other than dunder del.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.295
So, in this video, let's check out&nbsp;`__del__`.

00:00:43.295 --> 00:00:46.745
See what it's supposed to do, 
 hone in on some of those pitfalls.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:50.240
And, of course,&nbsp;see some 
 more robust alternatives at the end.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.440
First up, how does `__del__`&nbsp;relate to the `del` keyword?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.810
Well, there are three ways 
 that you can use the&nbsp;`del` keyword.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.401
You can say `del x[something]`.

00:00:59.401 --> 00:01:03.360
And that usage corresponds 
 exactly&nbsp;to calling the `__delitem__` method.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.185
You can call it with `del x.something`.

00:01:06.185 --> 00:01:10.094
And that&nbsp;corresponds 
 exactly to the `__delattr__` method.

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:16.056
Finally, there's just `del x`, which 
 does not call&nbsp;the `__del__` method.

00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:19.128
This is our first hint 
 about why people don't like `del`.

00:01:19.128 --> 00:01:21.740
`__delitem__`&nbsp;and `__delattr__` are 
 very straightforward.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.131
Nobody hates those.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.327
`str(x)` calls `__str__`

00:01:25.327 --> 00:01:27.435
`repr(x)` calls `__repr__`

00:01:27.435 --> 00:01:29.445
`len(x)` calls `__len__`

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:31.954
But `del x` does not call `__del__`.

00:01:31.954 --> 00:01:33.988
But it kind&nbsp;of looks like it does.

00:01:33.988 --> 00:01:37.119
If I run this example, I see the `__del__` printout.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:40.100
But if I comment out&nbsp;`del x`, it still gets called.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.168
Let's throw in some print statements at the start,

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:47.190
just before we call&nbsp;`del`, and at the end, 
 so we see exactly when this `__del__` is being called.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.174
We see start, then just&nbsp;before 
 `del`, then the `__del__` printout, and then end.

00:01:52.174 --> 00:01:55.054
So, it seems like `del` is triggering&nbsp;`__del__`.

00:01:55.054 --> 00:01:59.765
When we comment out `__del__`, it appears 
 that it's getting called after the end&nbsp;of the function.

00:01:59.765 --> 00:02:03.238
So, is this like manual 
 versus explicit garbage collection?

00:02:03.238 --> 00:02:04.366
Well, still no.

00:02:04.366 --> 00:02:07.336
If we&nbsp;create another variable `y` and assign it to `x`,

00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:09.548
then even if we manually delete `x`,

00:02:09.548 --> 00:02:13.181
we don't see&nbsp;the `__del__` 
 until after the end of the function.

00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:15.982
`__del__` is, of course, short for "delete."

00:02:15.982 --> 00:02:20.123
This&nbsp;function is supposed 
 to run before the object is deleted.

00:02:20.123 --> 00:02:25.067
But there's a big difference between&nbsp;the 
 object itself and the name that you assigned to it.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:28.580
Every object in Python has 
 what's called a&nbsp;reference count.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.433
The reference count tells you how 
 many things are referencing that object.

00:02:32.433 --> 00:02:36.841
Assigning&nbsp;a name like `x` to point 
 to an object increases its reference count.

00:02:36.841 --> 00:02:39.694
If I create another 
 name&nbsp;like `y` for the same object,

00:02:39.694 --> 00:02:41.837
that also increases the reference count.

00:02:41.837 --> 00:02:46.064
So, what the `del` keyword is&nbsp;doing 
 here is actually just deleting the name `x`.

00:02:46.064 --> 00:02:49.666
This effectively just reduces the 
 reference count&nbsp;of the object by one.

00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:53.250
But we don't want to delete the 
 object if there are still things referencing&nbsp;it.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.885
So, `__del__` is actually supposed to 
 be run when the object's ref count hits zero.

00:02:57.885 --> 00:03:02.848
If we&nbsp;didn't have the `y` variable, then 
 `del x` here deletes the only reference to the object.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:05.920
So its&nbsp;ref count goes to 
 zero, and it should be deleted.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.816
That's why `del` sometimes calls `__del__`.

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:10.828
But that's kind of just circumstantial.

00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:13.895
But even once you understand 
 that `del x` doesn't&nbsp;directly call `__del__`,

00:03:13.895 --> 00:03:17.014
there's still a lot of confusion around `__del__`.

00:03:17.014 --> 00:03:20.055
You might think that&nbsp;`__del__` 
 is a great place to put cleanup code.

00:03:20.055 --> 00:03:24.086
Maybe I open up a file descriptor, and&nbsp;
 then when nobody's using it anymore, close it.

00:03:24.086 --> 00:03:29.092
The main problem with this idea is that&nbsp;
 the documentation for `__del__` explicitly states

00:03:29.092 --> 00:03:31.716
that it may just never be called.

00:03:31.716 --> 00:03:33.990
Not&nbsp;a great property for cleanup code.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.964
The first reason `__del__` may never 
 be called is&nbsp;because of reference cycles.

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:39.898
Suppose I have an `x` and a `y`.

00:03:39.898 --> 00:03:43.620
Maybe it's a tree structure, so `x`&nbsp;
 has some children and `y` is one of them.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.961
Then, I include a back 
 reference to `x` as the parent of&nbsp;`y`.

00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:49.440
So, `x`'s children have a reference to `y`,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.765
And then `y` has a reference back up to `x`.

00:03:51.765 --> 00:03:53.424
This&nbsp;is called a reference cycle.

00:03:53.424 --> 00:03:58.733
And when you have a reference cycle, it's 
 impossible for either of&nbsp;the ref counts of `x` or `y` to hit zero.

00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:02.877
Even after this function returns. And it's 
 impossible&nbsp;to access `x` or `y` ever again

00:04:02.877 --> 00:04:04.533
because they were just local variables.

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:08.040
Their ref counts won't go to&nbsp;
 zero because they're still referencing each other.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.685
Furthermore, even if an 
 object's ref count hits&nbsp;zero,

00:04:11.685 --> 00:04:17.312
Python explicitly states that that doesn't 
 guarantee the object's `__del__` is called at that&nbsp;time.

00:04:17.312 --> 00:04:21.653
Now, in CPython, which is the 
 Python that 99% of you are going to be using,

00:04:21.653 --> 00:04:25.301
CPython does&nbsp;call `__del__` 
 immediately when the ref count hits zero.

00:04:25.301 --> 00:04:30.783
And for reference cycles, there's a garbage&nbsp;collector 
 which periodically runs and detects these kinds of cycles.

00:04:30.783 --> 00:04:33.436
As long as the interpreter&nbsp;didn't flat-out crash,

00:04:33.436 --> 00:04:37.250
CPython does a really good job of 
 ensuring that the garbage collector runs

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.116
and all of your objects' 
 `__del__` methods are called.

00:04:40.116 --> 00:04:42.790
But still, the docs insist 
 that this might&nbsp;not happen.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.444
So you can't really depend on it.

00:04:44.444 --> 00:04:48.501
Oh, and by the way, since the time 
 that&nbsp;`__del__` is called isn't guaranteed,

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:53.749
it's basically impossible for you to be able to&nbsp;
 handle any errors that might propagate out of them.

00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:59.440
Therefore, the interpreter just completely&nbsp;
 ignores exceptions raised in a `__del__` method.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.681
It'll still print the traceback.

00:05:01.681 --> 00:05:04.640
But as you&nbsp;can see, it still ran the code afterward.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.479
Oh yeah, and remember, if `__del__` is called&nbsp;at all,

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:10.994
it might be called while 
 the interpreter is shutting down.

00:05:10.994 --> 00:05:13.689
This `__del__` tries to&nbsp;
 dump some state into a JSON file.

00:05:13.689 --> 00:05:17.537
Remember, exceptions get ignored if 
 anything goes&nbsp;wrong trying to open this file.

00:05:17.537 --> 00:05:21.496
And then, if Python happens to wait until 
 the interpreter&nbsp;is shutting down to call this `__del__`,

00:05:21.496 --> 00:05:25.778
then this `json` module, which is a 
 global variable,&nbsp;may have already been deleted.

00:05:25.778 --> 00:05:31.443
In CPython, in practice, using 
 even a&nbsp;relatively oldish version like 3.7,

00:05:31.443 --> 00:05:33.630
I haven't actually run into&nbsp;this issue.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.310
But once again, the documentation 
 won't make any promises

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.217
And says you need to watch out for this.

00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:41.520
What else could there possibly 
 be&nbsp;that's more confusing about `del`?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.798
With all the lack of promises 
 about even&nbsp;basic functionality,

00:05:44.798 --> 00:05:47.496
you wouldn't think `del` 
 would support that many features.

00:05:47.496 --> 00:05:52.524
But it actually purposefully supports 
 the idea of so-called resurrecting an&nbsp;object

00:05:52.524 --> 00:05:54.880
that's about to be deleted.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.383
Let's say this `__del__` method is called

00:05:56.383 --> 00:06:00.662
because&nbsp;there are no more outside 
 accessible references to the `self` variable.

00:06:00.662 --> 00:06:02.844
Well, we can just put `self`&nbsp;into a global variable.

00:06:02.844 --> 00:06:05.744
Thereby increasing its 
 reference count back up to 1.

00:06:05.744 --> 00:06:08.683
Of course, it's kind&nbsp;of your 
 own fault if you do something like this.

00:06:08.683 --> 00:06:11.319
But Python guarantees you 
 are allowed to&nbsp;do this.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:13.912
If you increase the ref 
 count of an object inside `__del__`,

00:06:13.912 --> 00:06:16.891
then its memory&nbsp;won't be 
 reclaimed by the garbage collector.

00:06:16.891 --> 00:06:18.969
It will survive until another day.

00:06:18.969 --> 00:06:22.486
But&nbsp;you know they're not letting you get away 
 without throwing another curveball in&nbsp;there, right?

00:06:22.486 --> 00:06:25.287
If an object is resurrected, then when it dies again,

00:06:25.286 --> 00:06:29.559
its `__del__` method&nbsp;may 
 be called, or it might not be called.

00:06:29.559 --> 00:06:33.581
So, you also need to make sure that your 
 `__del__`&nbsp;doesn't do anything nasty if it gets called twice.

00:06:33.581 --> 00:06:36.430
I already mentioned that exceptions 
 from `__del__`&nbsp;get ignored, right?

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.541
Okay, so how about we just get to an example

00:06:38.541 --> 00:06:42.560
where it does something useful&nbsp;
 and it should theoretically be okay to use?

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.248
This class makes a temporary directory.

00:06:45.248 --> 00:06:48.529
When you&nbsp;create an instance, it 
 creates an actual temp file.

00:06:48.529 --> 00:06:50.553
You can manually call `remove` on it.

00:06:50.553 --> 00:06:53.187
Or&nbsp;the file is removed when it's garbage collected.

00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:56.173
Notice that the first time we 
 call `remove`, it&nbsp;sets `name` to `None`.

00:06:56.173 --> 00:06:58.696
This means that if `remove` 
 is called multiple times,

00:06:58.696 --> 00:07:01.949
either by the user or&nbsp;
 by `__del__` being called multiple times,

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:04.237
nothing will happen after the first time.

00:07:04.237 --> 00:07:07.306
We're definitely&nbsp;depending 
 here on CPython's unadvertised property

00:07:07.306 --> 00:07:09.792
that garbage collection will happen before&nbsp;shutdown.

00:07:09.792 --> 00:07:15.268
And it will happen early enough in 
 shutdown that this module, `shell_util`, will&nbsp;still exist.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:17.698
I'm definitely not confident given all those restrictions.

00:07:17.698 --> 00:07:21.359
But it does seem to work&nbsp;with the 
 current version of Python that I'm using.

00:07:21.359 --> 00:07:24.102
So, if `__del__` is so finicky, 
 it might never&nbsp;get called,

00:07:24.102 --> 00:07:28.912
it might get called twice, it ignores 
 exceptions, globals might no longer exist.

00:07:28.912 --> 00:07:32.833
How&nbsp;am I supposed to reliably 
 clean up the resources used in my code?

00:07:32.833 --> 00:07:36.702
The most robust solution is&nbsp;to use a `with` statement.

00:07:36.702 --> 00:07:38.360
Define `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.200
And make sure that you&nbsp;call your 
 cleanup code in the `__exit__` method.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.853
Then you can use the class like this.

00:07:42.853 --> 00:07:45.886
We just&nbsp;say `with`, we create a new instance as `d`.

00:07:45.886 --> 00:07:48.326
Do whatever we need to do inside the `with`&nbsp;block.

00:07:48.326 --> 00:07:52.715
Then when the `with` block is over, 
 Python guarantees that the `remove` method is&nbsp;called.

00:07:52.715 --> 00:07:55.806
The behavior of the `with` statement 
 is much more precisely defined.

00:07:55.806 --> 00:07:57.572
And you can&nbsp;depend on it.

00:07:57.572 --> 00:07:59.082
This is slightly limiting though.

00:07:59.082 --> 00:08:01.145
We have to use the `with` statement.

00:08:01.145 --> 00:08:03.648
What if I&nbsp;don't know when 
 I want to delete my temp file?

00:08:03.648 --> 00:08:05.439
Maybe I'm making a text editor.

00:08:05.439 --> 00:08:10.066
And I want&nbsp;to delete this temp file 
 whenever the user is not looking at it anymore.

00:08:10.066 --> 00:08:12.375
That might&nbsp;not be confinable to a `with` block.

00:08:12.375 --> 00:08:16.509
But certainly, if Python has no more 
 references&nbsp;to the object, then it's fine to delete.

00:08:16.509 --> 00:08:19.503
If you really want to support cases 
 where you&nbsp;can't use `with` statements,

00:08:19.503 --> 00:08:21.142
there is another alternative.

00:08:21.142 --> 00:08:22.493
Let's leave our `__enter__` and&nbsp;`__exit__` methods.

00:08:22.493 --> 00:08:24.966
We still want to encourage people 
 to do it the right way.

00:08:24.966 --> 00:08:26.917
Go ahead and delete&nbsp;the `__del__`.

00:08:26.917 --> 00:08:31.454
Then use the `weakref` module 
 to create a finalizer for our object.

00:08:31.454 --> 00:08:34.648
We can manually&nbsp;
 call the finalizer in our `remove` method.

00:08:34.648 --> 00:08:36.703
It's even fine to call it multiple times.

00:08:36.703 --> 00:08:41.240
We can also&nbsp;check if a finalizer has 
 already run by checking its `alive` property.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.563
A finalizer does have some&nbsp;
 of the same pitfalls as `__del__`.

00:08:44.563 --> 00:08:47.703
In particular, they have the 
 same exception-ignoring behavior.

00:08:47.703 --> 00:08:52.811
However, Python makes much stronger 
 guarantees that finalizers will run and when they'll run.

00:08:52.811 --> 00:08:55.081
In particular, when the interpreter shuts down,

00:08:55.081 --> 00:08:57.250
all remaining finalizers that are still alive

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:59.638
and haven't been disabled will be called.

00:08:59.638 --> 00:09:02.954
And they'll be called in the 
 reverse order that&nbsp;they were created in.

00:09:02.954 --> 00:09:06.749
It's also guaranteed that these 
 finalizers will run early in the shutdown&nbsp;process,

00:09:06.749 --> 00:09:10.014
before any global variables 
 like modules have been deleted.

00:09:10.014 --> 00:09:13.328
And, of course, by design,&nbsp;finalizers 
 are allowed to be called multiple times.

00:09:13.328 --> 00:09:16.052
And their effects will only happen the&nbsp;
 first time they're called.

00:09:16.052 --> 00:09:17.534
This makes them a more robust

00:09:17.534 --> 00:09:22.510
and dependable solution compared to&nbsp;
`__del__` when you can't use a `with` statement.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.600
I didn't really say much about&nbsp;
 what weak references are.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.484
Normal references, like 
 assigning the name `x` to&nbsp;a variable,

00:09:28.484 --> 00:09:30.027
increase the reference count.

00:09:30.027 --> 00:09:31.355
Those are called strong references.

00:09:31.355 --> 00:09:33.858
And they would keep&nbsp;
 the object from being garbage collected.

00:09:33.858 --> 00:09:38.664
But we obviously don't want the finalizer 
 of our object&nbsp;to keep it from being garbage collected.

00:09:38.664 --> 00:09:42.329
Its whole purpose is to run 
 right before the object is&nbsp;garbage collected.

00:09:42.329 --> 00:09:44.822
So, it's actually possible 
 to create so-called weak references

00:09:44.822 --> 00:09:48.407
that don't&nbsp;increase the reference count 
 and don't prevent it from being garbage collected.

00:09:48.407 --> 00:09:50.836
If you want to hear&nbsp;more about 
 them, make sure to leave a comment.

00:09:50.836 --> 00:09:51.559
That's all I've got.

00:09:51.559 --> 00:09:52.393
See you in the next one.

00:09:52.393 --> 00:09:58.480
And don't forget to slap that 
 like&nbsp;button an odd number of times.

